I am currently trying to take my original pet to heaven in Slash'EM,
and it is proving to be a very difficult challenge, so I thought I'd
post about it and see if anybody could give me some tips.  Note that I
am discussing the current version of Slash'EM (7E7F2) where monster
polymorph is temporary - I already ascended with my original pet in an
older version by polymorphing it into a trapper - owing to a bug, it
stayed polymorphed for the rest of the game.

Healers seem like a good choice for this challenge because they are
well equipped to look after their pet, but they have the problem that
even a large cat or dog has only a 33% chance of leaving a corpse.
Knights have the advantage that a grown horse always leaves a corpse,
and is easier to keep under control, but the disadvantage that a dead
horse is too heavy to drag to an ice box until it can be resurrected.
Other possible classes which I haven't experimented with much are Ice
Mages (a grown winter wolf always leaves a corpse) and Fire Mages
(hell hounds are very tough for a starting pet, and have fire
resistance which could come in handy in Gehennom, but are no more
likely to leave a corpse than an ordinary dog).

Polymorphing the pet is very useful in this challenge, but never
permanent unless it gets slimed (IIRC), and while a green slime always
leaves a corpse, it doesn't seem like much of a pet otherwise.  If the
pet is polymorphed into an intelligent monster (what is the
probability of this happening per polymorph?) then you can give it an
AoLS, and I believe that it will keep it until it dies for real (or do
monsters regard any other amulet as better?).

Not all methods of polymorph work in the same way.  IIRC, zapping your
pet with a wand of polymorph carries a 4% risk that it will die from
system shock leaving no corpse, but feeding it a chameleon corpse does
not.  Is there a risk of system shock when a monster is hit by a
genetic engineer's polymorph attack?  If not, one nifty strategy might
be to play a doppelganger, polymorph oneself into a genetic engineer,
and attack one's own pet to keep it polymorphed - assuming that you
could guarantee not to do enough physical damage to kill it.

It occurs to me that there are ways of dying where the monster never
leaves a corpse.  For example, disintegration is a hazard unless black
dragons have been genocided.  Is there a complete list of ways of
dying and never leaving a corpse, and can they all be eliminated?
Eva.

